.TH nroff "" "Command"
.SH NAME
nroff \- text processor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBnroff [\-adiTx] [\-\fIm\fBname\fR] [\-\fIn\fBN\fR] [\-\fIr\fBaN\fR] [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
nroff -- text processor
Usage:	nroff [ option ... ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-d	Debugging mode
	-i	Read standard input after each 'file' has been read
	-mname	Read macro package /usr/lib/tmac.name
	-nN	Number first page of output 'N' (default: 1)
	-raN	Set number register 'a' to value 'N'
	-x	Do not eject to bottom of final page
.HE
.B nroff
formats text for a printing terminal or line printer.
It reads each
.I file
in turn, interprets the formatting requests and macros embedded in
it, and writes the formatted result to the standard output; a
request or macro call is a line beginning with
.B .
or an apostrophe,
followed by a two-letter (or user-defined) name and optional
arguments (a leading apostrophe suppresses the automatic break that a
leading
.B .
would otherwise cause).
With no
.I file
arguments,
.B nroff
reads the standard input.
.PP
Text lines are filled and adjusted (words are packed onto a line up
to the current line length, with extra space inserted between them
to justify the right margin) unless no-fill mode has been requested.
Some of the more commonly used requests are:
.DS
.ta 8n
\fB.br\fR	break the output line here
\fB.sp \fIN\fR	space down \fIN\fR lines
\fB.fi\fR / \fB.nf\fR	enter fill / no-fill mode
\fB.ce \fIN\fR	center the next \fIN\fR lines
\fB.ta \fIN ...\fR	set tab stops
\fB.ig\fR	ignore input until a matching \fB..\fR
\fB.de \fIxx\fR	define macro \fIxx\fR (ended by \fB..\fR)
\fB.ds \fIxx str\fR	define string \fIxx\fR
\fB.nr \fIR N\fR	set number register \fIR\fR to \fIN\fR
\fB.so \fIfile\fR	read \fIfile\fR in place of this line
\fB.tm \fImsg\fR	print \fImsg\fR on the standard error
.DE
.PP
A macro package such as
.B \-ms
supplies a higher-level set of macros (paragraphs, headings, and the
like) built out of these requests; this repository's own
.B \-man
package,
.BR tmac.an ,
is one such package, used to format manual pages.
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Force ASCII-approximation of special characters; normally set
automatically because
.B nroff
(unlike its typesetting counterpart) approximates special characters
by default.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Print each request as it is executed, for debugging macros.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
After each
.I file
has been read to completion, also read and format the standard
input.
.IP "\fB\-m\fIname\fR"
Read the macro package
.BI /usr/lib/tmac. name
before processing any
.IR file .
.IP "\fB\-n\fIN\fR"
Number the first page of output
.IR N ,
instead of 1.
.IP "\fB\-r\fIaN\fR"
Set number register
.I a
(a single character) to the value
.IR N .
.IP \fB\-T\fR
Set the predicate register normally used to tell whether the output
is destined for a typesetter, even though running as
.BR nroff .
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Do not eject to the bottom of the page after the last line of the
last file.
.SH FILES
.B /usr/lib/tmac.*
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B deroff
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B nroff
prints a diagnostic on the standard error and, for most errors,
continues processing; unrecoverable conditions (such as running out
of memory for a macro or string, or an unbalanced
.BR .ie / .el )
cause it to abort.
